1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:04,787
The most quoted sentence in the history of American spaceflight was never actually transcribed.

2
00:00:04,787 --> 00:00:08,080
The people whose job it was to write it down said it could not be done.

3
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:13,837
You have heard it in every documentary about the Apollo One fire. We've got a bad fire.

4
00:00:13,837 --> 00:00:15,520
Let's get out. We're burning up.

5
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:20,515
Here is what the official Review Board wrote about that transmission.

6
00:00:20,515 --> 00:00:25,422
The entire second transmission is garbled and is, therefore, subject to wide variation of

7
00:00:25,422 --> 00:00:28,510
interpretation. No definitive transcription is possible.

8
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,748
So the Board did something almost no retelling mentions.

9
00:00:32,748 --> 00:00:37,701
It printed three different readings of the same five seconds, side by side, and declined to

10
00:00:37,701 --> 00:00:38,790
choose between them.

11
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:44,570
Every video you have seen picks one of the three and calls it the transcript. This one will not.

12
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:51,156
Because when you read the three thousand pages the investigators actually produced, the story

13
00:00:51,156 --> 00:00:55,160
is not the one you were told. It is stranger, and harder to sit with.

14
00:00:56,390 --> 00:00:59,924
A test that nobody in the building had classified as dangerous.

15
00:00:59,924 --> 00:01:05,140
A hatch that was designed to be hard to open, on purpose, for a reason that was written down.

16
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:08,202
Ten point three seconds of voice.

17
00:01:08,202 --> 00:01:12,690
And a finding that no single cause of the fire was ever conclusively identified.

18
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:16,332
This is not the story of how three men died.

19
00:01:16,332 --> 00:01:21,444
It is the story of what the investigation found, in its own words, and how far that is from

20
00:01:21,444 --> 00:01:22,680
what everyone repeats.

21
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:25,440
You're watching mindwired.

22
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:30,130
Apollo One. The transcript that doesn't exist.

23
00:01:31,330 --> 00:01:34,796
To understand how this happened, start with a piece of paper.

24
00:01:34,796 --> 00:01:37,750
The paper that decided whether a test was dangerous.

25
00:01:38,950 --> 00:01:42,536
The formal name was the Space Vehicle Plugs-Out Integrated Test.

26
00:01:42,536 --> 00:01:47,859
Spacecraft zero one two, sitting on top of a Saturn One B rocket at Launch Complex thirty four,

27
00:01:47,859 --> 00:01:50,100
with no propellant in the rocket at all.

28
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:56,051
Its purpose was to run the vehicle in as near a flight configuration as practical.

29
00:01:56,051 --> 00:02:00,280
Simulated fuel-cell power. The ground umbilicals disconnected. Plugs out.

30
00:02:01,490 --> 00:02:06,606
The crew would be sealed inside, on internal power, running a countdown to a launch that would

31
00:02:06,606 --> 00:02:10,470
not happen. It was a rehearsal, and rehearsals had a rule about danger.

32
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:13,952
Here is the rule, in the Board's words.

33
00:02:13,952 --> 00:02:19,161
This Plugs-Out Test had not been classified as hazardous because only those tests involving

34
00:02:19,161 --> 00:02:24,026
fueled vehicles, hypergolic propellants, cryogenic systems, high pressure tanks, live

35
00:02:24,026 --> 00:02:28,490
pyrotechnics or altitude chamber tests were routinely classified as hazardous.

36
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:31,116
Read that list again.

37
00:02:31,116 --> 00:02:36,699
Fuel, hypergolics, cryogenics, high-pressure tanks, pyrotechnics, altitude chambers.

38
00:02:36,699 --> 00:02:42,150
The plugs-out test had none of them. By the rule as written, it was not hazardous.

39
00:02:43,390 --> 00:02:44,825
The Board's Finding Five.

40
00:02:44,825 --> 00:02:50,050
Those organizations responsible for the planning, conduct and safety of this test failed to

41
00:02:50,050 --> 00:02:54,126
identify it as being hazardous. And its determination, in one sentence.

42
00:02:54,126 --> 00:02:56,710
The test conditions were extremely hazardous.

43
00:02:57,950 --> 00:03:02,054
Frank Borman, the only astronaut on the Board, told the Senate in April.

44
00:03:02,054 --> 00:03:04,049
I did not consider it as hazardous.

45
00:03:04,049 --> 00:03:09,351
I do not believe that anyone within the test organization or the program office considered it

46
00:03:09,351 --> 00:03:13,170
hazardous. And, this is the unfortunate trap through which we fell.

47
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:16,867
The Senate named no one, and neither will we.

48
00:03:16,867 --> 00:03:22,116
It concluded that NASA's long history of successes in testing and launching space vehicles with

49
00:03:22,116 --> 00:03:27,366
pure oxygen environments at sixteen point seven p s i and lower pressures led to overconfidence

50
00:03:27,366 --> 00:03:28,250
and complacency.

51
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:31,454
Now the detail almost nobody tells.

52
00:03:31,454 --> 00:03:35,909
The emergency egress practice was in the test at the flight crew's own request.

53
00:03:35,909 --> 00:03:40,534
They asked for it in September, because the next test, the fully fuelled countdown

54
00:03:40,534 --> 00:03:45,440
demonstration, was classified hazardous, and they wanted to have rehearsed getting out.

55
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:48,564
One more thing about the paperwork.

56
00:03:48,564 --> 00:03:53,252
A two hundred and nine page revision to the test procedure arrived at half past five the

57
00:03:53,252 --> 00:03:56,501
evening before, with four more pages the morning of the test.

58
00:03:56,501 --> 00:04:01,455
The Board found this prevented people becoming adequately familiar with it, and found, in the

59
00:04:01,455 --> 00:04:05,610
same breath, that it did not contribute to the accident. Both halves are true.

60
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:11,191
So on the afternoon of the twenty seventh, nobody in the white room at the top of that gantry

61
00:04:11,191 --> 00:04:13,984
was standing next to something they believed was dangerous.

62
00:04:13,984 --> 00:04:16,350
What they were standing next to was an atmosphere.

63
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:20,111
The number that decided everything was a pressure.

64
00:04:20,111 --> 00:04:23,030
And the reason for that pressure was completely sensible.

65
00:04:24,230 --> 00:04:24,914
Finding Two.

66
00:04:24,914 --> 00:04:30,270
The test was conducted with a sixteen point seven pounds per square inch absolute, one hundred

67
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:34,315
percent oxygen atmosphere. In flight, the cabin would have run at five.

68
00:04:34,315 --> 00:04:37,050
On the pad it ran at more than three times that.

69
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:42,880
Why so high? Because the cabin was deliberately run about two pounds above the air outside.

70
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:47,509
That overpressure pushed the plug-type inner hatch into its seal from the inside, and it let

71
00:04:47,509 --> 00:04:52,240
engineers find leaks, because anything leaking would flow outward, where they could detect it.

72
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:54,216
Hold onto that.

73
00:04:54,216 --> 00:04:59,080
The same pressure that sealed the hatch is the pressure that would make it impossible to open.

74
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:04,407
But notice what the Board did not do. It did not blame oxygen alone.

75
00:05:04,407 --> 00:05:10,201
Finding Nine says a pure oxygen system presents severe fire hazards if, and the word is if, the

76
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:14,470
amount and location of combustibles are not restricted and controlled.

77
00:05:15,690 --> 00:05:21,251
On adding a second gas, the Board wrote that it would undoubtedly reduce the risk of fire to a

78
00:05:21,251 --> 00:05:22,375
significant degree.

79
00:05:22,375 --> 00:05:27,877
And then, in the next sentence, that it would introduce other operational problems and risks.

80
00:05:27,877 --> 00:05:33,320
That is the shape of this whole report. Every fix has a cost, and the Board wrote both down.

81
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:37,100
Here is what the pressure does to a spark.

82
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:42,665
In a sixteen and a half p s i oxygen atmosphere, the Board found, sparks blown from arcs can

83
00:05:42,665 --> 00:05:48,350
ignite combustible material several inches from the arc. Not touching it. Several inches away.

84
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:51,599
And now the root of the complacency.

85
00:05:51,599 --> 00:05:56,429
Spacecraft zero one two had already been pressurised with pure oxygen above atmospheric

86
00:05:56,429 --> 00:06:00,927
pressure four times before this test, for six hours and fifteen minutes in total.

87
00:06:00,927 --> 00:06:04,980
About two and a half times longer than it would be on the twenty seventh.

88
00:06:06,230 --> 00:06:11,223
It had always been fine. That is the sentence underneath every other sentence in this story.

89
00:06:11,223 --> 00:06:15,130
Nothing had ever happened before, so nothing was expected to happen now.

90
00:06:16,310 --> 00:06:18,968
Three hatches stood between the crew and the white room.

91
00:06:18,968 --> 00:06:22,100
The first one they would have to open was designed to open inward.

92
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:24,387
From the outside in.

93
00:06:24,387 --> 00:06:29,550
The boost protective cover hatch, which on that day was in place but not fully latched, because

94
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:32,866
the wire bundles run in for the test had distorted the cover.

95
00:06:32,866 --> 00:06:34,822
Then the ablative heat shield hatch.

96
00:06:34,822 --> 00:06:38,790
Then the inner pressure vessel hatch, the one the crew would reach first.

97
00:06:40,030 --> 00:06:41,604
The inner hatch was a plug.

98
00:06:41,604 --> 00:06:46,677
It sat in its opening like a stopper in a bottle, and it opened inward, into the cabin.

99
00:06:46,677 --> 00:06:48,310
Cabin pressure held it shut.

100
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:50,761
The Board put it plainly.

101
00:06:50,761 --> 00:06:55,627
With a slightly higher pressure inside the Command Module than outside, opening the inner hatch

102
00:06:55,627 --> 00:06:58,599
is impossible because of the resulting force on the hatch.

103
00:06:58,599 --> 00:07:03,209
Finding Four: due to internal pressure, the Command Module inner hatch could not be opened

104
00:07:03,209 --> 00:07:04,080
prior to rupture.

105
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:10,087
And the determination that follows it, which is the hardest sentence in the report.

106
00:07:10,087 --> 00:07:13,360
The crew was never capable of effecting emergency egress.

107
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:20,565
How long did the hatches take under normal conditions? The Senate said at least ninety seconds.

108
00:07:20,565 --> 00:07:26,269
The hatch panel gave forty seconds from inside and seventy from outside, under ideal conditions.

109
00:07:26,269 --> 00:07:31,974
Borman, asked if ninety was right, said approximately, it depends upon the training of the crew.

110
00:07:31,974 --> 00:07:35,420
There is no one clean number, so we will not give you one.

111
00:07:36,650 --> 00:07:40,270
Now the part that makes this a tragedy and not simply negligence.

112
00:07:40,270 --> 00:07:44,056
A quick-opening hatch had been considered, and rejected, on purpose.

113
00:07:44,056 --> 00:07:48,845
Accidental release in space was considered to impose a risk in excess of the benefits.

114
00:07:48,845 --> 00:07:54,079
The Senate's words: these items had not been omitted through neglect, but because of decisions

115
00:07:54,079 --> 00:07:58,200
based on an attempt to balance one potential set of risks against another.

116
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:01,051
The reasoning was not hidden.

117
00:08:01,051 --> 00:08:06,080
NASA's own history records that the contractor had been told not to design an explosive hatch,

118
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:11,162
because a hatch that blew accidentally in orbit meant an instant vacuum and certain death for a

119
00:08:11,162 --> 00:08:13,195
crewman not wearing his pressure suit.

120
00:08:13,195 --> 00:08:17,100
They weighed a death in space against a death on the pad, and they chose.

121
00:08:17,100 --> 00:08:18,330
And they wrote it down.

122
00:08:19,570 --> 00:08:21,113
There is a further twist.

123
00:08:21,113 --> 00:08:26,793
An outward-opening hatch for the later Block Two spacecraft was already in design before the

124
00:08:26,793 --> 00:08:30,250
fire. Not for escape. For the convenience of spacewalks.

125
00:08:31,460 --> 00:08:35,283
Borman gave the House his opinion, and it is an opinion, not a Board finding.

126
00:08:35,283 --> 00:08:39,950
If they had had a hatch that opened outward and opened in two seconds, there is no question in

127
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:41,787
my mind that they would have escaped.

128
00:08:41,787 --> 00:08:46,405
He added that it was his firm opinion the crew would have escaped with a hatch that opened in

129
00:08:46,405 --> 00:08:47,100
eight seconds.

130
00:08:48,310 --> 00:08:53,530
Doctor Van Dolah, who led the fire panel, told the Senate that with a two to three second hatch

131
00:08:53,530 --> 00:08:58,475
the crew could have escaped with only minor injuries at most. Again, a witness's judgment.

132
00:08:58,475 --> 00:09:03,530
The Board itself never made that finding. Hold both of those thoughts as we go to the clock.

133
00:09:04,770 --> 00:09:06,932
The Board's clock runs on Greenwich time.

134
00:09:06,932 --> 00:09:10,888
Six thirty in the evening at the Cape is twenty three thirty on the record.

135
00:09:10,888 --> 00:09:12,470
Here is what the record holds.

136
00:09:13,660 --> 00:09:17,231
Eighteen hundred Greenwich, one in the afternoon at the Cape.

137
00:09:17,231 --> 00:09:21,271
Gus Grissom enters the spacecraft, then Roger Chaffee, then Ed White.

138
00:09:21,271 --> 00:09:25,310
Grissom in the left couch, White in the centre, Chaffee on the right.

139
00:09:26,550 --> 00:09:31,313
Twenty minutes later, a hold. Grissom reports a sour smell in the suit oxygen loop.

140
00:09:31,313 --> 00:09:35,790
It is investigated. The Board would later find it was not related to the fire.

141
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:41,921
Nineteen forty five, the hatches are installed and the cabin is purged to pure oxygen.

142
00:09:41,921 --> 00:09:47,207
Around twenty two forty, another hold, for a live microphone that will not switch off in the

143
00:09:47,207 --> 00:09:52,033
communications loop. At twenty three twenty, the count holds at T minus ten minutes.

144
00:09:52,033 --> 00:09:53,010
It never resumes.

145
00:09:54,240 --> 00:09:58,987
Twenty three thirty and fifty five seconds. A momentary failure in the electrical power.

146
00:09:58,987 --> 00:10:02,440
The Board would later call this the probable moment of ignition.

147
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:06,934
Nine point seven seconds later, the first transmission.

148
00:10:06,934 --> 00:10:11,861
The Board calls it an exclamatory remark, and says this transmission is not clear.

149
00:10:11,861 --> 00:10:16,667
Listeners believe this initial remark was Hey, or Fire, but this is not certain.

150
00:10:16,667 --> 00:10:19,010
Laboratory analysis pointed to Grissom.

151
00:10:20,310 --> 00:10:26,398
One and a half seconds after that, the transmission that is clear. Got a fire in the cockpit.

152
00:10:26,398 --> 00:10:30,456
Preceded by a word the Board could not resolve, I've or We've.

153
00:10:30,456 --> 00:10:35,693
Many listeners believed it was Chaffee, the pilot, whose job was communications.

154
00:10:35,693 --> 00:10:38,900
The Board wrote: no firm conclusion can be drawn.

155
00:10:40,090 --> 00:10:43,130
Then six point eight seconds of nothing on the voice loop.

156
00:10:43,130 --> 00:10:45,960
Six point eight seconds in which the record is silent.

157
00:10:47,420 --> 00:10:50,256
Twenty three thirty one and sixteen point eight seconds.

158
00:10:50,256 --> 00:10:54,104
The second transmission, five seconds long. This is the one everyone quotes.

159
00:10:54,104 --> 00:10:58,863
On screen are the Board's three readings, exactly as it printed them, and we will not read one

160
00:10:58,863 --> 00:11:00,382
aloud as if it were the truth.

161
00:11:00,382 --> 00:11:04,990
Bell Telephone Laboratories told the Board there would be nearly as many interpretations of

162
00:11:04,990 --> 00:11:07,370
what was said as there are qualified listeners.

163
00:11:09,060 --> 00:11:10,117
That is everything.

164
00:11:10,117 --> 00:11:15,400
From the first word to the last, the crew's voice on the record totals ten point three seconds.

165
00:11:16,850 --> 00:11:21,743
At about twenty three thirty one and nineteen seconds the cabin ruptures from the pressure.

166
00:11:21,743 --> 00:11:26,529
At twenty three thirty one and twenty two point four seconds, all voice and all data end.

167
00:11:26,529 --> 00:11:31,530
Van Dolah's estimate was that the fire itself probably lasted only about twenty five seconds.

168
00:11:32,750 --> 00:11:38,120
The Board reconstructed the crew's last actions in one sentence, and one sentence is all we

169
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:43,549
will use. Grissom had left his couch. White had reached for the hatch, as he was trained to.

170
00:11:43,549 --> 00:11:47,030
Chaffee had stayed on communications, as he was trained to.

171
00:11:48,280 --> 00:11:50,043
Now the people this story forgets.

172
00:11:50,043 --> 00:11:53,516
Outside the spacecraft, the pad crew were thrown back by the flash.

173
00:11:53,516 --> 00:11:58,181
They came back with extinguishers and were working on the hatches about a minute after the

174
00:11:58,181 --> 00:11:59,270
first report of fire.

175
00:12:00,470 --> 00:12:06,453
The white room filled with smoke. Visibility, in the record's words, was virtually zero.

176
00:12:06,453 --> 00:12:12,980
Five men took turns going in, working the hatches by feel, coming out to breathe, going back in.

177
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:19,815
Four of them are named on the House record. Donald Babbitt. James Gleaves. L. D.

178
00:12:19,815 --> 00:12:24,306
Reece, of North American. Henry Rogers, of NASA quality control.

179
00:12:24,306 --> 00:12:29,500
Congressman Daddario put on the record that they acted with great courage.

180
00:12:30,730 --> 00:12:34,305
At about twenty three thirty six, all three hatches were open.

181
00:12:34,305 --> 00:12:39,782
Roughly five minutes after the first word. The crew's voice had lasted ten point three seconds.

182
00:12:39,782 --> 00:12:41,800
The hatches had taken five minutes.

183
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:48,100
Sixty eight days after the fire, the Review Board handed the Administrator its report.

184
00:12:48,100 --> 00:12:51,990
It knew where the fire probably began. It could not prove what lit it.

185
00:12:53,180 --> 00:12:57,333
The Board was created the night of the fire, by Deputy Administrator Robert Seamans.

186
00:12:57,333 --> 00:13:01,684
It convened the next morning at the Kennedy Space Center, under Doctor Floyd Thompson of

187
00:13:01,684 --> 00:13:02,080
Langley.

188
00:13:03,260 --> 00:13:05,156
Its only astronaut was Frank Borman.

189
00:13:05,156 --> 00:13:08,580
His job was to take apart the spacecraft his friends had died in.

190
00:13:09,770 --> 00:13:15,880
Twenty one task panels. Eleven findings. And when it was bound, nearly three thousand pages.

191
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:21,753
Borman would later say to a congressman, with some edge in it, perhaps you haven't had time to

192
00:13:21,753 --> 00:13:24,510
read the three thousand pages. Here is what is in them.

193
00:13:25,670 --> 00:13:28,898
Spacecraft zero one two was taken apart piece by piece.

194
00:13:28,898 --> 00:13:33,123
Before anything was removed, its position was photographed and recorded.

195
00:13:33,123 --> 00:13:36,820
The Board wanted the cabin to testify before the engineers did.

196
00:13:38,070 --> 00:13:40,134
Finding one, in the Board's own words.

197
00:13:40,134 --> 00:13:45,132
There was a momentary power failure at twenty three thirty and fifty five seconds, Greenwich

198
00:13:45,132 --> 00:13:49,315
Mean Time. Evidence of several arcs was found in the post-fire investigation.

199
00:13:49,315 --> 00:13:52,900
No single ignition source of the fire was conclusively identified.

200
00:13:54,530 --> 00:13:55,715
Then the determination.

201
00:13:55,715 --> 00:14:00,610
The most probable initiator was an electrical arc, near the floor, in the lower forward section

202
00:14:00,610 --> 00:14:05,144
of the left-hand equipment bay, where instrumentation power wiring for the Environmental

203
00:14:05,144 --> 00:14:10,090
Control System leads into the space between the Environmental Control Unit and the oxygen panel.

204
00:14:11,290 --> 00:14:15,728
And the last sentence of finding one, which the Board thought necessary to write down.

205
00:14:15,728 --> 00:14:18,360
No evidence was discovered that suggested sabotage.

206
00:14:19,590 --> 00:14:20,786
Now the spot itself.

207
00:14:20,786 --> 00:14:26,229
Low on the left side, under a small access door for the lithium hydroxide canister, a power

208
00:14:26,229 --> 00:14:29,818
cable ran where the door's inside edge could rub against it.

209
00:14:29,818 --> 00:14:32,330
The insulation was vulnerable to abrasion.

210
00:14:33,510 --> 00:14:38,531
In a pure oxygen cabin at that pressure, the Board had already established what a small arc

211
00:14:38,531 --> 00:14:43,220
could do. Sparks blown from arcs can ignite combustible material several inches away.

212
00:14:44,470 --> 00:14:48,171
And then the Board wrote the most honest sentence in the whole report.

213
00:14:48,171 --> 00:14:53,036
If this cable were the cause, it cannot be proven, since both the power cable and the inside

214
00:14:53,036 --> 00:14:55,310
edge of the door were completely destroyed.

215
00:14:56,820 --> 00:15:00,490
The fire had destroyed the only two objects that could have convicted it.

216
00:15:00,490 --> 00:15:04,210
So the investigators did the other thing. They worked out what it was not.

217
00:15:05,390 --> 00:15:10,611
Electrostatic discharge, ruled out. Spontaneous combustion, ruled out.

218
00:15:10,611 --> 00:15:16,353
Mechanical heat, impact, and resonance heating. All examined. All eliminated.

219
00:15:16,353 --> 00:15:21,350
What remained was electrical, and it remained probable, not proven.

220
00:15:22,570 --> 00:15:24,869
The disassembly turned up something else.

221
00:15:24,869 --> 00:15:27,954
Inside the spacecraft, the Board found a wrench socket.

222
00:15:27,954 --> 00:15:32,440
It was logged as an enclosure to the report. Nobody had meant to leave it there.

223
00:15:33,620 --> 00:15:38,325
The Board also noted that coolant leakage from the plumbing had been a chronic problem, and

224
00:15:38,325 --> 00:15:41,945
that the coolant was both corrosive and combustible. It stopped there.

225
00:15:41,945 --> 00:15:44,220
It did not build a chain it could not prove.

226
00:15:45,450 --> 00:15:51,706
So when the Board summed up, it did not name a cause. It named conditions. A sealed cabin.

227
00:15:51,706 --> 00:15:57,057
A pure oxygen atmosphere. Extensive combustible materials. Vulnerable wiring.

228
00:15:57,057 --> 00:16:03,730
Vulnerable coolant plumbing. Inadequate provisions for escape. Inadequate provisions for rescue.

229
00:16:04,970 --> 00:16:08,624
Any one of them, alone, might have been survivable.

230
00:16:08,624 --> 00:16:12,780
Together, in that cabin, on that afternoon, they were not.

231
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:18,389
And then one sentence that was aimed at everyone, including the men who wrote it.

232
00:16:18,389 --> 00:16:23,481
In its devotion to the many difficult problems of space travel, the Apollo team failed to give

233
00:16:23,481 --> 00:16:27,870
adequate attention to certain mundane but equally vital questions of crew safety.

234
00:16:29,450 --> 00:16:34,424
The Board had answered how. Congress wanted to know who had been told what, and when.

235
00:16:34,424 --> 00:16:38,930
The answer turned up in a report Nasa's Administrator said he had never seen.

236
00:16:40,150 --> 00:16:41,005
Two committees.

237
00:16:41,005 --> 00:16:46,304
In the Senate, Aeronautical and Space Sciences, under Clinton Anderson, sitting from February

238
00:16:46,304 --> 00:16:49,324
nineteen sixty seven to January nineteen sixty eight.

239
00:16:49,324 --> 00:16:54,510
In the House, the Subcommittee on Nasa Oversight, under Olin Teague, through April and May.

240
00:16:55,710 --> 00:17:00,827
In December nineteen sixty five, more than a year before the fire, General Phillips had led a

241
00:17:00,827 --> 00:17:03,578
review of the contractor, North American Aviation.

242
00:17:03,578 --> 00:17:07,980
His cover letter to the company's president is dated the nineteenth of December.

243
00:17:09,210 --> 00:17:12,003
The committee put the review's purpose on the record.

244
00:17:12,003 --> 00:17:15,376
A task force review, headed by Apollo Program Director Samuel C.

245
00:17:15,376 --> 00:17:19,697
Phillips, made a detailed examination of the contractor's management and made many

246
00:17:19,697 --> 00:17:24,598
recommendations for remedial action, in order to return the program to an acceptable level of

247
00:17:24,598 --> 00:17:25,231
performance.

248
00:17:25,231 --> 00:17:30,027
Its results went to North American management on the nineteenth of December, nineteen sixty

249
00:17:30,027 --> 00:17:30,290
five.

250
00:17:31,550 --> 00:17:36,329
On the twenty seventh of February nineteen sixty seven, one month after the fire, Senator

251
00:17:36,329 --> 00:17:39,551
Mondale asked in open hearing whether such a report existed.

252
00:17:39,551 --> 00:17:43,740
Administrator Webb replied that he knew of no unusual General Phillips report.

253
00:17:44,940 --> 00:17:45,561
It existed.

254
00:17:45,561 --> 00:17:50,810
And the Senate, when it had read it, made two findings that have to be held at the same time.

255
00:17:51,990 --> 00:17:52,315
First.

256
00:17:52,315 --> 00:17:57,351
The task force findings had no effect on the accident, did not lead to the accident, and were

257
00:17:57,351 --> 00:18:02,170
not related to the accident. Second. The committee believes it should have been informed.

258
00:18:03,690 --> 00:18:06,206
Mondale added his own views to the report.

259
00:18:06,206 --> 00:18:11,719
He wrote of evasiveness, of a lack of candor, and of a patronizing attitude toward Congress.

260
00:18:11,719 --> 00:18:13,037
That is in the record.

261
00:18:13,037 --> 00:18:17,710
What is also in the record is that the Phillips report did not start the fire.

262
00:18:18,890 --> 00:18:20,240
Then there is Thomas Baron.

263
00:18:20,240 --> 00:18:24,590
He was a quality control inspector for North American at the Kennedy Space Center, from

264
00:18:24,590 --> 00:18:27,640
September nineteen sixty five to November nineteen sixty six.

265
00:18:28,840 --> 00:18:33,911
He wrote up what he saw as quality failures on the Apollo work. Fifty five pages of them.

266
00:18:33,911 --> 00:18:35,734
He gave the report to the press.

267
00:18:35,734 --> 00:18:40,805
On the fifth of January nineteen sixty seven, three weeks before the fire, North American

268
00:18:40,805 --> 00:18:41,660
discharged him.

269
00:18:42,910 --> 00:18:45,045
North American answered it point by point.

270
00:18:45,045 --> 00:18:49,824
But some of the company's own officials later told Congress that about half of Baron's charges

271
00:18:49,824 --> 00:18:50,790
were well grounded.

272
00:18:51,990 --> 00:18:56,875
Baron testified before the House subcommittee on the twenty first of April nineteen sixty seven.

273
00:18:56,875 --> 00:19:00,030
By then he was expanding his report toward five hundred pages.

274
00:19:01,210 --> 00:19:06,107
Under questioning, he said a North American technician knew exactly what caused the fire.

275
00:19:06,107 --> 00:19:08,418
A man he had met by chance in a drugstore.

276
00:19:08,418 --> 00:19:12,930
He admitted he had taken this to a newspaperman, not to Nasa and not to the Board.

277
00:19:14,130 --> 00:19:15,502
The technician was called.

278
00:19:15,502 --> 00:19:19,514
He testified that Baron gets all his information from anonymous phone calls.

279
00:19:19,514 --> 00:19:21,520
On that point, the committee moved on.

280
00:19:22,760 --> 00:19:28,362
Six days later, on the twenty seventh of April nineteen sixty seven, Thomas Baron, his wife and

281
00:19:28,362 --> 00:19:33,020
his stepdaughter were killed near Mims, Florida, when a train struck their car.

282
00:19:34,210 --> 00:19:38,527
Nasa's own history of the program gives it one word of comment. Ironically.

283
00:19:38,527 --> 00:19:42,960
No official document says anything more than that, and neither will this one.

284
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:49,494
When the Senate finally wrote its report, in January nineteen sixty eight, it began with this.

285
00:19:49,494 --> 00:19:52,171
No single person bears all of the responsibility.

286
00:19:52,171 --> 00:19:57,360
It happened because many people made the mistake of failing to recognize a hazardous situation.

287
00:19:58,930 --> 00:20:04,312
What changed was not one part. It was the definition of what counted as dangerous.

288
00:20:04,312 --> 00:20:06,740
Nasa gave the Senate the list itself.

289
00:20:07,920 --> 00:20:13,392
First item on Nasa's list. Every test in a pure oxygen atmosphere was redefined as hazardous.

290
00:20:13,392 --> 00:20:18,217
The rule that had let the plugs-out test through, because there was no fuel and no

291
00:20:18,217 --> 00:20:19,570
pyrotechnics, was gone.

292
00:20:20,810 --> 00:20:23,380
Responsibility for test procedures was redefined.

293
00:20:23,380 --> 00:20:26,055
An independent Office of Flight Safety was created.

294
00:20:26,055 --> 00:20:29,517
Emergency training and pad fire and rescue equipment were rebuilt.

295
00:20:29,517 --> 00:20:32,350
Every crewed flight moved to the Block Two spacecraft.

296
00:20:33,530 --> 00:20:38,246
Combustible materials inside the cabin were, in Nasa's words, severely restricted and

297
00:20:38,246 --> 00:20:38,856
controlled.

298
00:20:38,856 --> 00:20:44,071
Nasa told the Senate this one change was more significant than any other improvement resulting

299
00:20:44,071 --> 00:20:45,070
from the accident.

300
00:20:46,300 --> 00:20:51,368
Behind that sentence sits a materials program of more than three thousand tests on more than

301
00:20:51,368 --> 00:20:55,224
five hundred materials. Nylon went out. Beta fibre and Teflon came in.

302
00:20:55,224 --> 00:20:59,300
Soldered aluminium oxygen lines were replaced with brazed stainless steel.

303
00:21:00,490 --> 00:21:05,923
And the hatch. Two hatches became one. The unified hatch opened outward, in about five seconds.

304
00:21:05,923 --> 00:21:09,240
And it opened regardless of the pressure inside the cabin.

305
00:21:10,480 --> 00:21:12,396
Remember why the old one could not.

306
00:21:12,396 --> 00:21:17,378
A slightly higher pressure inside than outside, and opening the inner hatch was impossible.

307
00:21:17,378 --> 00:21:21,320
That sentence, from the Board, is the one this hatch was built to erase.

308
00:21:22,550 --> 00:21:27,300
Emergency oxygen for the crew. Launch facilities modified for rapid egress.

309
00:21:27,300 --> 00:21:28,947
And the atmosphere itself.

310
00:21:28,947 --> 00:21:34,583
On the pad, the cabin would now carry sixty percent oxygen and forty percent nitrogen, at

311
00:21:34,583 --> 00:21:40,410
roughly sea-level pressure. In flight, it stayed pure oxygen at five pounds per square inch.

312
00:21:41,610 --> 00:21:46,589
I want to be careful here, because there is a version of this story where the fire becomes a

313
00:21:46,589 --> 00:21:51,080
necessary sacrifice, and I do not think the record supports saying it that cleanly.

314
00:21:51,080 --> 00:21:52,920
What the record does hold is this.

315
00:21:54,140 --> 00:21:59,323
The Senate, in January nineteen sixty eight, wrote that it was the committee's hope that the

316
00:21:59,323 --> 00:22:04,676
remainder of the program would be carried out with greater understanding and dedication than if

317
00:22:04,676 --> 00:22:07,380
there had been no accident. A hope. Not a claim.

318
00:22:08,600 --> 00:22:14,050
And Frank Borman, in nineteen ninety nine, asked whether the changes were what let Nasa recover

319
00:22:14,050 --> 00:22:17,549
so fast, answered in one word and one comparison. Absolutely.

320
00:22:17,549 --> 00:22:22,540
Just take a look at the difference in recovery time between the Apollo and the Shuttle.

321
00:22:23,770 --> 00:22:27,280
The mission was designated Apollo Two Oh Four. It never flew.

322
00:22:27,280 --> 00:22:32,746
The crew's widows asked Nasa to reserve the name for the flight that never left the pad, and on

323
00:22:32,746 --> 00:22:36,831
the twenty fourth of April nineteen sixty seven, Nasa made it official.

324
00:22:36,831 --> 00:22:39,880
The flight that never flew kept its name. Apollo One.

325
00:22:41,610 --> 00:22:46,064
Grissom, White and Chaffee are three of the twenty one astronauts who never came home.

326
00:22:46,064 --> 00:22:50,985
If the investigation into their deaths held you, the other eighteen stories are already on this

327
00:22:50,985 --> 00:22:51,399
channel.

328
00:22:51,399 --> 00:22:56,009
Watch Twenty One Astronauts Never Came Home, Here's Every Story, right here on mindwired.

329
00:22:56,009 --> 00:23:00,360
And subscribe to mindwired, so you don't miss the next case we open from the record.

